\DOC X86_VERBOSE_STEP_TAC

\TYPE {X86_VERBOSE_STEP_TAC : thm * (thm option array) -> string -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Symbolically executes one x86 machine instruction from the current state in an
{eventually}-style goal, replacing the current state variable by a freshly
named successor state and adding assumptions describing that successor.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument {(exth1,exth2) : thm * (thm option array)} pairs the
code-length theorem for the program being executed with an array of per-offset
decoding theorems; together they supply the instruction to step. The second
argument {sname : string} names the new state variable introduced for the
post-instruction state.

The goal's conclusion must have the form {eventually x86 P t}, where {t} is the
current state; it becomes {eventually x86 P u} with {u} a new state variable
named by {sname}. Among the assumptions, the {bytes_loaded} fact and the
accumulated {MAYCHANGE ... s0 t} fact about {t} are discharged and replaced by
the corresponding facts about {u}, the {MAYCHANGE} term being extended with the
components the instruction writes; the previously known component-read facts
about {t} are re-asserted for {u}; and new assumptions are added giving the
instruction's effects — the updated program counter, updated registers, vector
registers, flags or memory locations, and where applicable the event-trace
update. The assumptions about {t} itself are retained.
{
   A, bytes_loaded ... t, MAYCHANGE ... s0 t, reads(t) ?- eventually x86 P t
   ================================================================  X86_VERBOSE_STEP_TAC (exth1,exth2) sname
   A, bytes_loaded ... t, MAYCHANGE ... s0 t, reads(t),
      bytes_loaded ... u, MAYCHANGE ... s0 u, reads(u), effects(u)
        ?- eventually x86 P u
}

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces only this single kind of transformation occurs, always
on goals whose conclusion is an {eventually x86} statement about a concrete
state variable.

\ENDDOC
